# Architecture Layering v0.1

Status: Active

Related decision: [0001. Adopt Agent-legible Repository Harness](../decisions/0001-adopt-agent-legible-repository-harness.md)

## 1. Purpose

{{ project_name }} should stay legible as it grows. The main risk is not missing abstraction on day one; it is letting domain rules, API details, persistence, generated contracts, runtime state, and UI behavior blur together until small changes become unsafe.

Layering rules describe dependency direction and ownership. They are not a demand for ceremony before the project needs it.

Before scaffolding a new stack or feature, write down the intended layer map and the DTO/source-of-truth strategy. It is cheaper to encode direction before code exists than to unwind reverse dependencies later.
{% if backend_stack == 'rust' %}
## 2. Rust Backend Layers

Target direction:

```text
Domain model -> Application use cases -> Adapters -> Interface/runtime
```

Suggested workspace shape:

```text
crates/
  core/   # pure domain types and state transitions
  app/    # use cases and port traits
  db/     # persistence adapters
  api/    # HTTP boundary, OpenAPI, runtime wiring
```

Rules:

- `core` must not depend on HTTP, SQL, provider SDKs, environment variables, or generated frontend types.
- `app` coordinates use cases through traits and should not depend on Axum request/response types or SQL query code.
- adapter crates may depend inward on domain/application traits.
- route handlers should translate HTTP to application calls and map results back to HTTP.

Rust source files under `crates/` should start with a short `//!` module boundary comment.
